%!testlib
% What the virtual memory account says, and what it does not say.
%
% Run through `xpost -d null` by run-ps-test.sh: prints SUCCESS iff every
% assertion holds.
%
% Three numbers describe a bank, and each answers a different question. They
% are easy to mistake for one another, and a reader who mistakes them draws
% the wrong conclusion from a perfectly healthy run, so they are pinned here.
%
%   used     the bytes below the bank's cursor. It rises as the bank takes
%            fresh storage, and an explicit reclaim winds it back: closing
%            the arena up slides the live entities together, so the cursor
%            ends where the live storage ends. A collection that runs of its
%            own accord does not wind it back, because it does not close the
%            arena up -- it puts space on a free list and leaves it where it
%            lies. So the difference in used across an ordinary operation is
%            the space that operation had to be given NEW because nothing
%            already released could serve it.
%
%   maximum  the storage the bank holds. It grows on demand and never
%            shrinks, so it is also the largest the bank has ever been. The
%            pages under the free storage are handed back to the system, but
%            the range they sat in is kept, so this figure does not move.
%
%   free     the bytes on the bank's free lists: recovered, still inside the
%            storage the bank holds, and available to an allocation of a size
%            they suit. Reported by .vmfreebytes. An explicit reclaim empties
%            them, because closing the arena up absorbs the blocks they named
%            -- the space is then above the cursor rather than on a list.
%
% The account an embedder wants is maximum less used: what the bank holds,
% less what is live in it. Before a reclaim the free lists hold the rest and
% .vmfreebytes reports it; after one the cursor has come back and the same
% space lies above it.
%
% COLLECTION: this reads the numbers around a collection it asks for itself,
% and asserts the direction each one moves rather than any absolute figure, so
% it does not depend on when a collection would otherwise have run.

/.vmfreebytes 1183615869 internaldict /.vmfreebytes get def
/used { vmstatus pop exch pop } bind def
/arena { vmstatus exch pop exch pop } bind def
/freebytes { .vmfreebytes pop } bind def

% ---- the three move only in the directions they are allowed to -----------

/u0 used def
/a0 arena def
/f0 freebytes def

a0 u0 ge (the storage a bank holds is at least the cursor into it) assert
f0 a0 le (the free lists cannot hold more than the bank does) assert

% Take a known amount, in pieces a single allocation can hold, then drop
% every reference to it and collect.
/hold 60 array def
0 1 59 { hold exch 60000 array put } for

/u1 used def
/a1 arena def
u1 u0 ge (taking storage does not lower the cursor) assert
a1 a0 ge (taking storage does not shrink the bank) assert
u1 u0 sub 2000000 gt (taking many arrays moves the cursor by a real amount) assert

/hold null def
1 vmreclaim

/u2 used def
/a2 arena def
/f2 freebytes def

% The cursor comes back. An explicit reclaim closes the arena up: the live
% entities are slid together and the cursor is left where they end, so the
% space the dropped arrays occupied is no longer below it. Asserted rather
% than merely described, so that a change to it is a failure here and not a
% surprise somewhere else.
u2 u1 le (an explicit reclaim lowers the cursor) assert

% The bank does not give its storage back, though. The pages under the free
% run are returned to the system, but the range they sat in is kept, so a
% context's largest moment is still the largest mapping it ever asks for.
a2 a1 ge (a reclaim does not shrink the bank) assert

% And it comes back by something like the amount that was dropped. This is
% the assertion that makes the first one readable: without it, "the cursor
% moved" would be indistinguishable from "the cursor moved by a byte". Sixty
% arrays of sixty thousand elements is at least fourteen million bytes at the
% narrower object width; the assertion asks for a quarter of that, which no
% rounding or bookkeeping difference reaches.
u1 u2 sub 3500000 gt
    (the cursor comes back by something like the space that was dropped) assert

% ---- and the account that follows from them ------------------------------
%
% After a reclaim the recovered space is above the cursor rather than on a
% list, so the free lists report little or nothing: the blocks they named
% were absorbed when the live entities were slid over them. The gap between
% what the bank holds and what is live in it is maximum less used, and it is
% the thing no single number here reports.

f2 f0 sub 3500000 lt
    (a reclaim does not leave the recovered space on the free lists) assert
a2 u2 sub 3500000 gt
    (what the bank holds is far more than what is live in it) assert
a2 f2 sub 0 ge (what a bank is using is not negative) assert

verdict
flush
